"On the action of the Weyl group on canonical bases (https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.08857)\nciteturn5academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.4 years ago; Title: On the action of the Weyl group on canonical bases ... Examples of this phenomenon include the action of separable permutations on the Kazhdan--Lusztig basis of irreducible representations for the symmetric group, and the action of separable elements of $W$ on dual canonical bases of weight zero in tensor product representations of a Lie algebra.\nTitle: On the action of the Weyl group on canonical bases\nAuthors: Fern Gossow, Oded Yacobi\nDate: Thu Jun 15 04:54:19 2023\n\nWe study representations of simply-laced Weyl groups which are equipped with canonical bases. Our main result is that for a large class of representations, the separable elements of the Weyl group $W$ act on these canonical bases by bijections up to lower-order terms. Examples of this phenomenon include the action of separable permutations on the Kazhdan--Lusztig basis of irreducible representations for the symmetric group, and the action of separable elements of $W$ on dual canonical bases of weight zero in tensor product representations of a Lie algebra. Our methods arise from categorical representation theory, and in particular the study of the perversity of Rickard complexes acting on triangulated categories.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn the action of the Weyl group on canonical bases | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2306.08857)\nciteturn5search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.7 years ago; Crawled: last month; # On the action of the Weyl group on canonical bases ... @misc{gossow2025actionweylgroupcanonical, title={On the action of the Weyl group on canonical bases}, author={Fern Gossow and Oded Yacobi}, year={2025}, eprint={2306.08857}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.RT}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.08857}, }\nSubmitted 25 Feb 2025\n\n# On the action of the Weyl group on canonical bases\n\nImageUniversity of SydneyImageCalifornia Institute of Technology\n\nFG\n\nFern Gossow\n\nOY\n\nOded Yacobi\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe study representations of simply-laced Weyl groups which are equipped with canonical bases. Our main result is that for a large class of representations, the separable elements of the Weyl group $W$W act on these canonical bases by bijections up to lower-order terms. Examples of this phenomenon include the action of separable permutations on the Kazhdan--Lusztig basis of irreducible representations for the symmetric group, and the action of separable elements of $W$W on dual canonical bases of weight zero in tensor product representations of a Lie algebra. Our methods arise from categorical representation theory, and in particular the study of the perversity of Rickard complexes acting on triangulated categories.\n\nView Paper\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{gossow2025actionweylgroupcanonical, title={On the action of the Weyl group on canonical bases}, author={Fern Gossow and Oded Yacobi}, year={2025}, eprint={2306.08857}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.RT}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.08857}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStrengthened colorability and antipodal-coloring conjectures for pseudocircle arrangements | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/354662/strengthened-colorability-and-antipodal-coloring-conjectures)\nciteturn5search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; An arrangement is diamond-free when no two triangles of its arrangement graph share an edge, and an antipodal 3-coloring of a great-pseudocircle arrangement is a 3-coloring in which antipodal points have the same color. ... Every simple diamond-free intersecting arrangement of $n\\geq 6$n≥6 pseudocircles is 3-colorable. ... These are experimental strengthenings of the Felsner–Hurtado–Noy–Streinu conjecture. ... Man-Kwun Chiu, Stefan Felsner, Manfred Scheucher, Felix Schröder, Raphael Steiner and Birgit Vogtenhuber, “Coloring circle arrangements: New 4-chromatic planar graphs”, arXiv:2205.08181 (2022).\n\n# Strengthened colorability and antipodal-coloring conjectures for pseudocircle arrangements\n\n4 years old · traced to 2022\n\nA pseudocircle arrangement is a collection of pseudocircles, and it is simple when no three pseudocircles meet at one point. It is intersecting when every pair of pseudocircles intersects. An arrangement is diamond-free when no two triangles of its arrangement graph share an edge, and an antipodal 3-coloring of a great-pseudocircle arrangement is a 3-coloring in which antipodal points have the same color.\n\nStrengthened pseudocircle-arrangement conjectures. The following three statements hold:\n\n  1. Every simple diamond-free intersecting arrangement of $n\\geq 6$n≥6 pseudocircles is 3-colorable.\n  2. Every simple intersecting arrangement of sufficiently many pseudocircles is 3-colorable.\n  3. Every simple arrangement of $n\\geq 7$n≥7 great-pseudocircles has an antipodal 3-coloring.\n\nThese are experimental strengthenings of the Felsner–Hurtado–Noy–Streinu conjecture. The paper reports computational evidence for them, but does not prove the three statements in general.\n\nReferences\n\n  1. Man-Kwun Chiu, Stefan Felsner, Manfred Scheucher, Felix Schröder, Raphael Steiner and Birgit Vogtenhuber, “Coloring circle arrangements: New 4-chromatic planar graphs”, arXiv:2205.08181 (2022).\n\ngraph-coloringplanar-graphspseudocircles\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n90  M.-K. Chiu et al. (https://real.mtak.hu/130866/1/2021_Book_ExtendedAbstractsEuroComb2021.pdf)\nciteturn5search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.2 years ago; Computations also suggest that sufficiently large great-pseudocircle arrangements have *antipodal colorings*, i.e., 3-colorings where antipodal points have the same color. Based on the experimental data we propose the following strengthened variants of Conjecture 1. ... (a) Every diamond-free intersecting arrangement of \\(n \\ge 6\\) pseudocircles is 3-colorable. ... (c) Every arrangement of \\(n \\ge 7\\) great-pseudocircles has an antipodal 3-coloring. ... **65**, 261–278 (2021)\n90  M.-K. Chiu et al.\n\nments, it is reasonable to think of it as a “hard” class for 3-coloring. The rationale for such thoughts is that triangles restrict the freedom of extending partial colorings. Our computational data indicates that sufficiently large intersecting pseudocircle arrangements that are *diamond-free*, i.e., no two triangles of the arrangement share an edge, are also 3-colorable. Computations also suggest that sufficiently large great-pseudocircle arrangements have *antipodal colorings*, i.e., 3-colorings where antipodal points have the same color. Based on the experimental data we propose the following strengthened variants of Conjecture 1.\n\n*Conjecture 3.* The following three statements hold.\n\n(a) Every diamond-free intersecting arrangement of \\(n \\ge 6\\) pseudocircles is 3-colorable.  \n(b) Every intersecting arrangement of sufficiently many pseudocircles is 3-colorable.  \n(c) Every arrangement of \\(n \\ge 7\\) great-pseudocircles has an antipodal 3-coloring.\n\n## References\n\n1. Aigner, M.: Graph Theory. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nColoring Circle Arrangements: (https://page.math.tu-berlin.de/~felsner/Paper/col-gpca-eucg.pdf)\nciteturn5search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; With Theorem 2 we gave a proof of Conjecture 1 for △-saturated great-pseudocircle arrange- ... also suggest that suﬃciently large great-pseudocircle arrangements have antipodal colorings, ... (a) Every diamond-free intersecting arrang